### Why the marker icons disappear

When you switch from **Mapbox GL JS** to **MapLibre GL JS** two things change that affect the code you posted:

| What you used in Mapbox GL JS | What happens in MapLibre GL JS (v4 + / v6) |
|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------|
| `map.loadImage(url, callback)` – the callback is **always** called (or with an error) | `map.loadImage(url)` now **returns a Promise** and **ignores the callback argument**. The callback you passed is therefore never executed, which is why you see the console warning. |
| The default `new mapboxgl.Marker()` draws the “pin” icon that lives in the **style’s sprite** (`style.sprite`). | If the style you are using does **not** contain a sprite (or the sprite URL still points to a `mapbox://` resource), the built-in pin disappears and you have to add your own image or use an HTML marker. |

So the two symptoms you see are actually the same root cause: the `loadImage` call never resolves, so the image never gets added to the style’s sprite, and the default marker can’t find the “pin” it expects.

---

## 1️⃣ Fix the `loadImage` call

Convert the old callback-style code to the Promise-style API that MapLibre now uses.

```js
// ❌ Old Mapbox style – will never call the callback in MapLibre
// map.loadImage(url, (err, img) => {
//   if (err) throw err;
//   map.addImage('pin', img);
// });

/* ✅ New MapLibre style – async/await (or .then) */
async function addPinImage(url) {
  try {
    // map.loadImage resolves to an object { data: ImageBitmap }
    const { data: imageBitmap } = await map.loadImage(url);
    // addImage expects the ImageBitmap (or HTMLImageElement)
    map.addImage('pin', imageBitmap);
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('Failed to load pin image:', e);
    // you can fall back to a built-in image or a placeholder here
  }
}

/* Call it after the map is ready */
map.on('load', () => {
  addPinImage('https://example.com/assets/pin.png');
});
```

If you prefer the classic `.then()` syntax:

```js
map.loadImage('https://example.com/assets/pin.png')
  .then(({ data }) => map.addImage('pin', data))
  .catch(err => console.error('loadImage error', err));
```

> **Tip:** `map.loadImage` works only after the map’s style has finished loading (i.e., inside a `map.on('styledata')` / `map.on('load')` handler). If you call it earlier you’ll get a rejected promise.

---

## 2️⃣ Make sure the style actually contains a sprite (or supply your own)

### 2.1. Using a style that already ships a sprite

If you are using a third-party style (e.g., the OpenFreeMap style shown in the migration guide) verify that its `sprite` property points to a **real URL** (not `mapbox://sprites/...`). Open the style JSON and look for something like:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sprite": "https://tiles.openfreemap.org/sprites/liberty",
  ...
}
```

If the `sprite` URL is missing or still uses `mapbox://`, the default pin icon cannot be found, and any `addImage('pin', …)` you do will never be used by the built-in `Marker`.

### 2.2. Adding your own image and using it in a custom marker

If you want full control (or you are using a style that has **no** sprite at all), you can:

1. **Load the image** with `map.loadImage` (as shown above) and register it with a custom name (`'my-pin'`).
2. **Create a marker that references that image** via a `SymbolLayer`, **or** use an **HTML marker**.

#### Example – Symbol-layer marker

```js
// After the image is added:
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-pin-layer',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: {
      type: 'FeatureCollection',
      features: [
        {
          type: 'Feature',
          geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.42, 37.78] },
          properties: {}
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  layout: {
    'icon-image': 'pin',   // the name you used in addImage()
    'icon-size': 1
  }
});
```

#### Example – HTML marker (no sprite needed)

```js
const el = document.createElement('div');
el.className = 'my-marker';
el.style.backgroundImage = 'url(https://example.com/assets/pin.png)';
el.style.width = '32px';
el.style.height = '32px';
el.style.backgroundSize = '100%';

new maplibregl.Marker(el)
  .setLngLat([-122.42, 37.78])
  .addTo(map);
```

> **Why this works:** An HTML marker does not rely on the style’s sprite at all, so you can keep the default style even if it has no sprite.

---

## 3️⃣ Quick checklist for “marker icons are gone”

| ✅ Done? | Action |
|--------|--------|
| **1** | Replace every `map.loadImage(url, cb)` with the Promise version (`await map.loadImage(url)` or `.then`). |
| **2** | Ensure the call happens **after** the map’s `load` (or `styledata`) event. |
| **3** | Verify the style you are using has a valid `sprite` URL that points to a **real HTTP(S) resource** (no `mapbox://`). |
| **4** | If you keep the default `new maplibregl.Marker()` without a custom element, make sure the style’s sprite contains an image named `pin` (or whatever name you register with `addImage`). |
| **5** | If you cannot or do not want to edit the style’s sprite, switch to an **HTML marker** (as shown above) or a **symbol layer** that uses the image you added with `addImage`. |
| **6** | Open the browser dev-tools → **Network** tab and look for the request to the sprite PNG/JSON. A 404 or a request to `api.mapbox.com` means the style still points at Mapbox resources. Fix the URL. |
| **7** | Check the console for any `Error: Image "pin" not found in style sprite` messages – they indicate the sprite is missing the key you are trying to use. |

